Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.680
This video is brought to you by Squarespace.
In a corner of SpaceX's headquarters in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.720
Hawthorne, California you won't find rocket&nbsp;
engineers or even adults for that matter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:16.000
For a number of years, Elon Musk ran a school out&nbsp;
of his rocket factory to educate his five children -&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.160
not including boy number six, Baby X. They had been&nbsp;
enrolled at a private school for gifted kids in LA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:26.720
but Musk wasn't satisfied with their education&nbsp;
as he once told the Chinese television network.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:31.120
The regular schools just they weren't doing the&nbsp;
things that I thought should be done. Musk has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:36.240
been critical of the American education system -&nbsp;
pointing out teachers don't explain why kids are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:41.360
taught a subject. They're taught a mathematical&nbsp;
formula without explaining why that formula is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.720
important. As an analogy, he says simply listing the&nbsp;
tools you need to take an engine apart isn't the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:51.920
same as trying to disassemble the engine yourself&nbsp;
and by doing so, learn the relevance of the tools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:56.960
as you go. When he's not happy with how things are&nbsp;
done, the entrepreneur tends to do something about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:03.200
it - whether it's creating rockets, cars, computer&nbsp;
chips, or his own school. He named it Ad Astra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:08.880
which is Latin for: to the stars. I actually hired&nbsp;
a teacher from the school they were at who also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:13.520
agreed with me that there was a better way to do&nbsp;
it. That teacher is Josh Dahn who I spoke with over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:18.000
Skype. Was it a tough decision for you to leave the&nbsp;
private school where you were teaching at to go on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:24.640
this new venture? No, of course not, right? I mean if&nbsp;
anything, you know, Elon asked me to think about it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.960
and I responded, you know, hours after we originally&nbsp;
spoke, like, in the middle of the night because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:33.360
it was so clearly a great opportunity. Ad&nbsp;
Astra was far from a typical school with no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:38.640
classes in language, music, sports, or even grade&nbsp;
levels. Students were grouped based on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.120
ability where they took part in complex team&nbsp;
games to test their critical thinking skills.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:48.640
In one simulation, teams competed at various art&nbsp;
auctions to try to assemble the best collections&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:56.432
to display at exhibitions around the world, similar to what these students are doing. I like&nbsp;the Shrine Gate. Okay, yeah, you do that one. 

00:01:56.432 --> 00:02:00.728
It's a lot for adults to juggle let alone the eight to 14-year-olds at Ad Astra.

00:02:00.728 --> 00:02:05.760
Musk reimagined how school could be taught&nbsp;
based on First Principles reasoning. A fancy way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:10.640
of saying: How could you rethink how something&nbsp;
is done in order to make it better? A strategy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.920
applied to Tesla's manufacturing of batteries. It&nbsp;
used to be very expensive to build batteries for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:20.800
electric cars. You could say that's just the way&nbsp;
it is and there's no way to bring the price down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:25.520
First Principles thinking would say it's possible&nbsp;
to make cheaper batteries by breaking it down to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:30.480
the basics: figuring out what the batteries are&nbsp;
made of and how those materials could be cleverly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:36.000
combined to form a battery cell. In education,&nbsp;
First Principles mean figuring out how students&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:41.600
can get the best learning experience possible to&nbsp;
bring about the best future for them and for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:46.000
world. I can't believe that it's sitting at a desk&nbsp;
listening to a teacher tell you how to you know do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:51.760
side angle side theorems in geometry class. Ad Astra&nbsp;
grew from nine kids in the first week of 2014&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:58.160
to 50 six years later - a mix of children of SpaceX&nbsp;
employees as well as high achievers from the area.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:02.960
Sometimes they ventured off-campus. A converted&nbsp;
garage at the former house of Gene Wilder once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:09.360
served as a chemistry lab. This unorthodox approach&nbsp;
to education doesn't come cheap. Tuition was around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:15.360
$30,000 a year with some receiving&nbsp;
financial aid. When Ad Astra closed in June 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.880
Musk donated funds to kickstart an online school&nbsp;
called Astra Nova headed by Josh Dahn. Although the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:26.080
SpaceX CEO isn't personally involved, he has&nbsp;
his fingerprints all over the new school. A&nbsp;

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:31.600
former SpaceX engineer teaches rocketry there. And&nbsp;
even the application is related to one of Musk's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:36.720
passions. Interested students have to submit&nbsp;
a response to this environmental problem. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:42.240
would be your answer? There's a large lake near a&nbsp;
small town. A corporation dumps harmful chemicals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:47.360
into the water. Scientists studying the lake say&nbsp;
it'll die in 10 years if the pollution continues.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:52.640
An individual, the puppet master, is concerned&nbsp;
new regulations will impact profits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:57.440
so they decide to pay a small group of scientists&nbsp;
to publish bogus research claiming the lake has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:02.800
never been healthier. The media reports on the&nbsp;
new study. Politicians have the power to stop the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:07.600
pollution but decide not to since there appears&nbsp;
to be disagreement over the health of the lake.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:13.200
Plus, the company employs so many people in town&nbsp;
its closure would devastate the economy. Voters who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:18.800
listen to the media, politicians, and scientists&nbsp;
are confused over what the truth actually is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:23.920
They re-elect the politicians. The corporation&nbsp;
continues dumping harmful chemicals into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:29.840
lake and the lake dies 10 years later. So, who is&nbsp;
to blame? There's no right or wrong answer but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:34.015
school is interested in how the children reason&nbsp;
through ethical dilemmas.

00:04:34.015 --> 00:04:40.223
It's not about preparing the 100 or so admitted students for an Ivy League education.&nbsp;In fact, Musk went on the record

00:04:40.223 --> 00:04:45.120
to say&nbsp;degrees don't matter when Tesla's artificial&nbsp;intelligence team &nbsp;looks to hire - 

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:48.349
putting it this&nbsp;way: Don't care if you even graduated high school.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.349 --> 00:04:53.229
Not to mention, many smart people dropped out&nbsp;
of college like Steve Jobs and Bill Gates.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.229 --> 00:04:58.429
Musk has said he simply wants evidence of&nbsp;
exceptional ability which he hopes can be fostered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.429 --> 00:05:03.949
at a young age. And SpaceX of course is an exercise&nbsp;
in possibility. The concepts are available to kids&nbsp;&nbsp;

